149
00:14:06,714 --> 00:14:12,157
[SPEAKER_02]: I, I think, maybe, whatever arising, all the pictures just take on self.

150
00:14:12,277 --> 00:14:17,020
[SPEAKER_02]: It means, like, just, I, I'm thinking that everything is in my control.

151
00:14:17,741 --> 00:14:19,362
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the meaning of self.

152
00:14:19,422 --> 00:14:21,063
[SPEAKER_02]: I, I feel into that divinity.

153
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:28,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, all of those pictures, all of those thoughts that are arising, they are arising in regard to a background.

154
00:14:29,808 --> 00:14:31,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where your sense of self is.

155
00:14:32,070 --> 00:14:39,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are not mindful, if you're not recognizing background to the extent that it is a background, means you're taking it to be yourself.

156
00:14:39,858 --> 00:14:42,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, whatever arises, arises for yourself.

157
00:14:43,528 --> 00:14:54,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're not necessarily thinking, this is me, this is mine, it's arising for me, in front of me, I can see, I can think about it, I, and always remains, I always remains the center of that experience.

158
00:14:55,456 --> 00:15:04,062
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you see that even that I is another thing that has arisen on that level of that background, that's a different story.

159
00:15:04,102 --> 00:15:10,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you see it as a background, you'll see, oh, well, background is only background in regard to something that it's right in front of you.

160
00:15:11,638 --> 00:15:13,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means background is determined by that thing.

161
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:15,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And vice versa.

162
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:17,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Background determines the actual.

163
00:15:18,202 --> 00:15:22,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can only arise... something can only arise directly based on the peripheral basis.

164
00:15:23,485 --> 00:15:28,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's mutually determining and you realize, well, wherever yourself goes, it will be either here or there.

165
00:15:29,049 --> 00:15:32,191
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no in-between or the third place that you don't know of.

166
00:15:32,371 --> 00:15:35,473
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either the background or the terrain, which means, oh, well, it's impermanent.

167
00:15:35,613 --> 00:15:40,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Because both of these things mutually determine each other, which means none of them can remain standing on their own.

168
00:15:41,287 --> 00:15:48,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that is the case, there is no room for the self, which is usually the implication of being able to stand by itself, alone.

169
00:15:50,271 --> 00:15:55,634
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean the background, standing, serving, indirect, as a receiver of all the things?

170
00:15:55,754 --> 00:16:00,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can call it whichever... yeah, you can call it... you can just say it's a background, because it's a background.

171
00:16:00,916 --> 00:16:05,318
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... you can... you can call it a more general, ambiguous, less defined thought.

172
00:16:06,679 --> 00:16:08,680
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of those, that's... that's a background.

173
00:16:10,296 --> 00:16:20,624
[SPEAKER_01]: When people start using the terms like the witness, the observer, the receiver, that already implies that they have taken that very much to be their self.

174
00:16:21,704 --> 00:16:23,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Because background is not witnessing anything.

175
00:16:23,606 --> 00:16:26,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Background is a background.

176
00:16:26,708 --> 00:16:30,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Background is only a background because it's not foreground.

177
00:16:31,772 --> 00:16:34,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And that which is foreground is a foreground because it's not a background.

178
00:16:36,512 --> 00:16:42,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you realize that, you recognize that all of the possibilities of your current foreground, that's already in the background.

179
00:16:43,454 --> 00:16:47,935
[SPEAKER_01]: The new things you can attend, based on the current things that you've been attending, and so on.

180
00:16:51,856 --> 00:16:53,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that way, you can't create anything.

181
00:16:53,437 --> 00:17:04,000
[SPEAKER_01]: You just find things already there, arisen in that background, which determines what's right in front of you, because you can attend it, as something that has arisen on account of that background.

182
00:17:05,713 --> 00:17:20,886
[SPEAKER_01]: But another way also works as well, because when you attend something directly right in front of you, that defines what it... well, what you're attending is that which is not the background, which means, in relation to that, everything else is the background.

183
00:17:21,907 --> 00:17:24,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... that's what Ñānavīrā was talking as well.

184
00:17:24,429 --> 00:17:27,431
[SPEAKER_01]: The... the actual determines the peripheral by not being it.

185
00:17:27,451 --> 00:17:34,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So... so he refers to the background or the general experience as a negative experience in regard to the positive thing you're attending.

186
00:17:35,238 --> 00:17:38,340
[SPEAKER_01]: But negative is determined only by the positive, by not being positive.

187
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:39,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the negative.

188
00:17:40,401 --> 00:17:44,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And positive is determined by that negative as well, by not being that negative.

189
00:17:45,844 --> 00:17:54,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why anything can become positive down around in front of you, or a negative form of determination.

190
00:17:55,930 --> 00:17:56,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Any of the aggregates.

191
00:17:57,031 --> 00:17:59,392
[SPEAKER_01]: It all depends what you're... how you're attending it.

192
00:18:01,333 --> 00:18:04,555
[SPEAKER_02]: When I contemplate something, I just feel like a...

193
00:18:05,976 --> 00:18:10,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually, like, this body is considered as, like, a container.

194
00:18:10,679 --> 00:18:13,060
[SPEAKER_02]: And, indeed, the things arising in the mind.

195
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:17,302
[SPEAKER_02]: And, just, just, this whole picture is just taken self.

196
00:18:17,362 --> 00:18:24,106
[SPEAKER_02]: If that is the case, then all this general, that the peripheral, these two things should be separated.

197
00:18:24,826 --> 00:18:27,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you, you, like, you're trying to separate them.

198
00:18:27,508 --> 00:18:34,832
[SPEAKER_01]: You, you, you're trying to separate them on a, on a direct, actual level, which means that's not really the background I'm talking about.

199
00:18:36,405 --> 00:18:41,830
[SPEAKER_01]: When you start comparing, oh, actual and peripheral, those are actually both actual for you.

200
00:18:42,891 --> 00:18:45,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because peripheral is only that which you're not attending.

201
00:18:46,514 --> 00:18:49,076
[SPEAKER_01]: The background of your attention, right there and then.

202
00:18:49,977 --> 00:18:54,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you want to look at it with your attention, it just becomes another actual.

203
00:18:54,981 --> 00:18:58,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And something else now is that which you're not looking at.

204
00:18:59,205 --> 00:19:02,868
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's learning how to see that without having to directly look at it.

205
00:19:04,058 --> 00:19:06,320
[SPEAKER_02]: How to perceive that it cannot?

206
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:07,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's... you can.

207
00:19:07,921 --> 00:19:08,441
[SPEAKER_01]: It's possible.

208
00:19:08,501 --> 00:19:12,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what yoniso manasikāra means.

209
00:19:12,845 --> 00:19:16,087
[SPEAKER_01]: The proper attention, depending how you translate it.

210
00:19:16,468 --> 00:19:19,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yoniso means the womb, the place of origin.

211
00:19:20,371 --> 00:19:24,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, usually, your attention pulls you out there at whatever you're attending.

212
00:19:24,954 --> 00:19:29,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But it does... it's almost like a form of direction, which means it has been directed.

213
00:19:30,138 --> 00:19:32,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And that place of origin, that...

214
00:19:33,272 --> 00:19:39,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Where that attention came from, that's something people are not mindful of, because that's the background of what they're attending.

215
00:19:41,275 --> 00:19:42,035
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the negative.

216
00:19:43,556 --> 00:19:47,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, your eye cannot see itself.

217
00:19:48,438 --> 00:19:52,619
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can discern it and know that it's there based on the things that you're seeing.

218
00:19:53,540 --> 00:19:54,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Eye, anything, sights.

219
00:19:55,681 --> 00:20:01,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, the fact that you already find your attention attending things, you can know

220
00:20:02,441 --> 00:20:02,901
[SPEAKER_01]: It's roots.

221
00:20:03,322 --> 00:20:05,344
[SPEAKER_01]: You can know where that direction came from.

222
00:20:05,984 --> 00:20:08,887
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no solid source that emits attention.

223
00:20:09,948 --> 00:20:17,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that there is no solid source that emits this attention is basically why people assume that there is a solid source, which is a sense of self.

224
00:20:19,516 --> 00:20:30,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Always, no matter what culture, what background people are, whenever there is a sense of self, it will always be the point of origin of my attention, my intention, my will, my volition,

225
00:20:30,967 --> 00:20:33,089
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inseparable from the sense of self.

226
00:20:34,430 --> 00:20:39,914
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to see that actually there is no solid point of it, it's just a direction.

227
00:20:39,954 --> 00:20:42,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of almost infinite in both directions.

228
00:20:42,196 --> 00:20:45,578
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't really come from a solid point.

229
00:20:45,598 --> 00:20:47,260
[SPEAKER_01]: You just find it there, attending.

230
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:49,501
[SPEAKER_01]: And then attending this, and then attending that.

231
00:20:50,922 --> 00:20:58,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can start to discern the origins of that direction, in a sense of the background of what's being attended by there and then.

232
00:21:00,350 --> 00:21:02,071
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what yoniso manasikāra is.

233
00:21:02,171 --> 00:21:09,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing things on a more general level, while you're attending something else, directly.

234
00:21:09,777 --> 00:21:16,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... so... and that recognition can't come, like, with that attention, so to speak.

235
00:21:16,662 --> 00:21:27,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said before, if you... if you're attending something, and then you just... you're aware of the background to some extent, but then if you look at it directly, it's not the background anymore.

236
00:21:28,110 --> 00:21:29,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's another direct thing.

237
00:21:30,165 --> 00:21:30,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

238
00:21:30,765 --> 00:21:38,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to learn how to see it peripherally, how to see it without moving your sight, without having to look at it.

239
00:21:38,751 --> 00:21:42,634
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'm aware of that already, it's already in front.

240
00:21:42,674 --> 00:21:43,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not necessarily.

241
00:21:43,795 --> 00:21:45,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in front when you attend it.

242
00:21:45,817 --> 00:21:49,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to learn how to be aware of it, because you are aware of it in a degree.

243
00:21:49,479 --> 00:21:50,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody is.

244
00:21:50,520 --> 00:21:53,562
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how intentions operate.

245
00:21:54,063 --> 00:21:59,707
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to learn how to remain aware without needing to attend it, or in this case, over-attend it.

246
00:22:01,226 --> 00:22:05,127
[SPEAKER_01]: In which, in which, which means, basically, it will not be that background anymore.

247
00:22:05,187 --> 00:22:10,649
[SPEAKER_01]: It will just be another thing that was kind of extracted from that background and now it's being fully attended.

248
00:22:13,510 --> 00:22:20,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the body posture and so on, these things are inherently almost always in the background.

249
00:22:21,072 --> 00:22:25,573
[SPEAKER_01]: And establishing mindfulness upon that will mean you're establishing mindfulness upon the background.

250
00:22:27,148 --> 00:22:32,229
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not more... it's not as much as a... as a container for your experience.

251
00:22:32,909 --> 00:22:39,511
[SPEAKER_01]: As you said, it's more... it's more like a necessary random cause for your experience.

252
00:22:40,711 --> 00:22:47,252
[SPEAKER_01]: The body, bodily organs, just the living body, it's there in the world, same as every other object you can see.

253
00:22:48,453 --> 00:22:55,334
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's a particular... it's slightly more specific object because it has a nature to be the reason for your experience as a whole.

254
00:22:55,937 --> 00:22:59,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there is no living body, there's nothing you can experience.

255
00:23:00,018 --> 00:23:02,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't even conceive experience without your living body.

256
00:23:03,698 --> 00:23:06,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the living body is an object in the world.

257
00:23:06,099 --> 00:23:08,559
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just compiled of matter, four great elements.

258
00:23:08,779 --> 00:23:14,600
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there, subject to elements, subject to aging, to getting sick, to dying, subject to everything.

259
00:23:15,180 --> 00:23:20,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as, you know, the stone is subject to weather, the animals are subject to other... Like, it's just out there.

260
00:23:21,581 --> 00:23:25,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it is the thing because of which your whole experience is there,

261
00:23:26,467 --> 00:23:30,129
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know it, whichever way you know it, including your body.

262
00:23:30,510 --> 00:23:38,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can talk about the body, and I think, because that body is already there, as that because of which you are able to designate things, including the body.

263
00:23:40,917 --> 00:23:54,546
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes I feel that there might be some meaning that some, of course, vipassana, that's actually quite not natural, but sometimes we are contemplating, sometimes we always imagine something like a

264
00:23:55,900 --> 00:23:57,640
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't really matter where it is happening.

265
00:24:25,833 --> 00:24:28,254
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that you are affected by what's happening.

266
00:24:29,195 --> 00:24:33,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Things can happen inside, it can happen inside, outside, it can happen inside and outside.

267
00:24:35,018 --> 00:24:38,180
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is they're happening and you're affected by that.

268
00:24:39,801 --> 00:24:41,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you want to be concerned with.

269
00:24:41,422 --> 00:24:47,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Not where it's happening, how, why, what reasons.

270
00:24:47,465 --> 00:24:53,728
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be concerned with the fact that you are affected by whatever is happening.

271
00:24:53,928 --> 00:24:54,809
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can...

272
00:24:55,307 --> 00:25:02,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, you overcome that by, again, by learning how to not exclude things out of your experience, whether direct, whether peripheral.

273
00:25:03,089 --> 00:25:06,169
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to... you have to develop mindfulness of your experience as a whole.

274
00:25:06,249 --> 00:25:18,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is the experience of an unpleasant person telling you something offensive, unpleasant, and... and you're emotionally upset on account of that, well, all of that is already one experience as a whole.

275
00:25:18,412 --> 00:25:23,933
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the living random body in the world that happens to be closest to you,

276
00:25:24,443 --> 00:25:30,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of which, you are what you are, and you are able to hear this person speak, and think on account of it, and get upset.

277
00:25:32,713 --> 00:25:40,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all simultaneous, because for as long as that living body there is present, means your whole experience will stand on it.

278
00:25:41,323 --> 00:25:41,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

279
00:25:45,487 --> 00:25:57,172
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as your experience... for as long as that living body is present, while it's present, everything else, the whole domain of the senses, thoughts, interaction with others, all that will stand.

280
00:25:57,932 --> 00:26:00,873
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no living body, none of this can stay even for a second longer.

281
00:26:01,674 --> 00:26:02,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It is simultaneous.

282
00:26:03,834 --> 00:26:14,919
[SPEAKER_01]: To start seeing it like that, eventually you will... well, you will... you will start recognizing inherently that you're not able to hold, you're not able to... because that random body there,

283
00:26:16,041 --> 00:26:19,243
[SPEAKER_01]: It's clearly not in your control and you don't possess that.

284
00:26:20,924 --> 00:26:32,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it is the reason of your experience, which means that... come here... which means that the very foundations of your experience are inaccessible to you.

285
00:26:33,691 --> 00:26:45,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means that your whole experience equally cannot be yours, including your volition, your intention, anything the closest associated to the sense of self,

286
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:51,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that as fundamentally undermined by the fact that you have no control over the four great elements.

287
00:26:51,543 --> 00:26:57,765
[SPEAKER_01]: They are basically the reason for the shape of your body, functionality of your body, life associated with it, consciousness paired with it.

288
00:26:58,726 --> 00:26:59,806
[SPEAKER_01]: That's removed.

289
00:27:00,287 --> 00:27:01,127
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is gone.

290
00:27:01,827 --> 00:27:02,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

291
00:27:02,447 --> 00:27:06,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about addressing, sorting out the person that upset you or anything.

292
00:27:06,529 --> 00:27:08,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just surmounting the whole picture.

293
00:27:09,591 --> 00:27:14,773
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in order to discern that actually, this could not have been yours to begin with.

294
00:27:16,520 --> 00:27:17,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

295
00:27:17,221 --> 00:27:19,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you will not be affected by whatever arises.

296
00:27:19,865 --> 00:27:23,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's, you know, the horrible experiences sometimes that we read about in the suttas.

297
00:27:24,432 --> 00:27:26,175
[SPEAKER_01]: But the arahants, they were not affected by it.

298
00:27:30,925 --> 00:27:42,715
[SPEAKER_02]: I think, but in the case of fututiona, how is it that sometimes it's important to the... like choosing the... the... thinking that, like, a content that thought has to... something has to choose.

299
00:27:42,735 --> 00:27:44,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, otherwise, I waste the time.

300
00:27:44,536 --> 00:27:53,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose if I keep thinking about the tree or something, and if I... Well, what you should... what you should do then, you should start thinking about yourself thinking about the tree.

301
00:27:54,884 --> 00:27:57,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Or thinking about yourself doing this.

302
00:27:57,106 --> 00:27:59,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Or thinking about yourself thinking something else.

303
00:27:59,943 --> 00:28:01,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just think that or do that.

304
00:28:02,804 --> 00:28:04,584
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you start developing mindfulness.

305
00:28:04,684 --> 00:28:06,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing yourself, do things.

306
00:28:06,244 --> 00:28:08,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing yourself, think things.

307
00:28:08,745 --> 00:28:13,706
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that long enough, then that self that's being seen will be recognized.

308
00:28:13,746 --> 00:28:15,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, this is just another thing that I am seeing.

309
00:28:17,987 --> 00:28:24,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, it is a phenomenon which means he's subject to all of those... it's subject to impermanence.

310
00:28:27,051 --> 00:28:41,559
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to keep seeing it like that because the tendency for the sense of self is to be... to have a privileged position in regard to your whole experience.

311
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:48,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you might see it as a thought, you might see it as a concept, you might see it as a phenomenon, but it's always assumed to be more.

312
00:28:48,804 --> 00:28:53,006
[SPEAKER_01]: But by constantly seeing yourself doing things, you're getting more familiar with it.

313
00:28:53,356 --> 00:28:57,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And being more familiar, you're seeing it clearer, and you realize there isn't anything more to this.

314
00:29:02,059 --> 00:29:08,620
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you be... how can you stop assuming consciousness, then, or... how can you be aware of consciousness?

315
00:29:08,740 --> 00:29:09,181
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

316
00:29:09,401 --> 00:29:13,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be aware of consciousness, because it's... it would be a contradiction in terms.

317
00:29:13,522 --> 00:29:14,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can discern.

318
00:29:15,062 --> 00:29:21,184
[SPEAKER_01]: You can know that consciousness is there, as in that direction I was talking about, in regard to the attention.

319
00:29:22,504 --> 00:29:27,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing the nature of direction means recognizing the nature of consciousness.

320
00:29:28,289 --> 00:29:31,933
[SPEAKER_01]: And consciousness is out there with that random matter.

321
00:29:33,274 --> 00:29:38,519
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that random matter, which I suppose consciousness, that your life is here known for what it is.

322
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:45,907
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people assume, again, it comes from that directionality of the sense of self as being the source for, you know, the

323
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:50,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Me speaking, me thinking, doing things and so on.

324
00:29:50,322 --> 00:30:02,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, whether it's me here, things out there, yonder, all of that is already there, together, because of that conscious body, which, in the sense of space, is secondary.

325
00:30:04,708 --> 00:30:06,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, there, here and there.

326
00:30:07,190 --> 00:30:08,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, here and yonder, here and yonder.

327
00:30:09,511 --> 00:30:10,991
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is already given there.

328
00:30:13,833 --> 00:30:16,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that there is that background I'm talking about.

329
00:30:17,289 --> 00:30:20,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not here, in here, as opposed to the objects around me.

330
00:30:21,031 --> 00:30:32,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Both are actually already there, given, and known as thoughts, as a representation, as dhamma, whatever you want to translate it.

331
00:30:32,677 --> 00:30:33,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, try to see that.

332
00:30:34,118 --> 00:30:34,838
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that...

333
00:30:36,459 --> 00:30:39,962
[SPEAKER_02]: That vipassana, that kind of practice, actually, it's mistaken.

334
00:30:40,002 --> 00:30:42,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, actually, there is something like a thinking.

335
00:30:42,624 --> 00:30:48,328
[SPEAKER_02]: This, this, this... some, some, some teachers, they teach, like, be mindful with the movement of the body.

336
00:30:48,728 --> 00:30:52,631
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, like, drawing the line between this body and outside.

337
00:30:52,751 --> 00:30:55,013
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's thought... it's already mistaken.

338
00:30:55,113 --> 00:30:59,877
[SPEAKER_01]: It will just be reinforcing the same mistake you began with.

339
00:31:00,657 --> 00:31:01,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just you don't want to be...

340
00:31:03,206 --> 00:31:04,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Drawing the line in between.

341
00:31:04,307 --> 00:31:10,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to actually be focusing constantly on what's right, what's wrong, what's actual, what's actual.

342
00:31:10,112 --> 00:31:15,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the harder you try to focus on that, the more oblivious of the background you will be.

343
00:31:15,296 --> 00:31:16,257
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not be seeing it.

344
00:31:17,117 --> 00:31:20,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means less and less foundation for mindfulness.

345
00:31:21,301 --> 00:31:28,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Which it's not accidental that all the people who seriously get into vipassana, they get very intense, very wound up.

346
00:31:30,355 --> 00:31:34,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're constantly just trying to catch it, trying to see it.

347
00:31:35,416 --> 00:31:51,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was just saying to you the other day, the simile in the suttas where the Buddha said, a person without the right view, he mismeditates, premeditates, overmeditates, undermeditates, says it's like a jackal waiting by the bins for the mouse to come out, or a bird waiting for the fish.

348
00:31:51,981 --> 00:31:54,841
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just praying, praying, praying, praying upon something.

349
00:31:56,082 --> 00:31:56,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Just waiting for it.

350
00:31:58,433 --> 00:31:59,974
[SPEAKER_01]: As an animal which is hunting.

351
00:32:00,734 --> 00:32:02,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a completely wrong attitude.

352
00:32:02,295 --> 00:32:03,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely directed out there.

353
00:32:04,677 --> 00:32:05,377
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no background.

354
00:32:05,397 --> 00:32:08,379
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no seeing yourself waiting for a mouse to come out of the bin.

355
00:32:09,980 --> 00:32:15,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were, you would recognize, well, whether you're looking for a mouse or whatever, it's utterly irrelevant.

356
00:32:15,163 --> 00:32:16,884
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to see myself, what I'm doing.

357
00:32:18,485 --> 00:32:21,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you can also start distinguishing wholesome and unwholesome.

358
00:32:21,487 --> 00:32:26,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Because usually, our mind is pulled by unwholesome things you don't really think about, maybe later.

359
00:32:26,700 --> 00:32:42,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you get used to seeing yourself doing things, thinking things, feeling things, whatever, you will then always have that, like a... an extra space to whatever has arisen, from which you can have a clearer point of view.

360
00:32:44,091 --> 00:32:48,773
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that, well, yeah, no matter how much I'm full or whatever, this seems inherently unwholesome.

361
00:32:49,674 --> 00:32:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I won't do it.

362
00:32:54,061 --> 00:32:56,082
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I think there is nothing to worry about.

363
00:32:56,102 --> 00:32:58,703
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, some people, they even just reading the book.

364
00:32:58,763 --> 00:33:04,226
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people, they worry that it was too much, like I was getting absorbed in it and... Why would you worry?

365
00:33:04,246 --> 00:33:09,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that random body, living body, paired with consciousness, is already there.

366
00:33:09,509 --> 00:33:12,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're reading the book or not, it's already there.

367
00:33:13,110 --> 00:33:19,213
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to learn how to maintain that knowledge without the need to be constantly bringing it to your mind.

368
00:33:21,935 --> 00:33:22,515
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you mean?

369
00:33:23,403 --> 00:33:32,668
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge of your random living code in there, which is the reason for your whole experience of reading the book, that knowledge is valid.

370
00:33:32,688 --> 00:33:33,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a real thing.

371
00:33:33,508 --> 00:33:34,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a basis for mindfulness.

372
00:33:35,770 --> 00:33:36,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the right knowledge.

373
00:33:36,890 --> 00:33:38,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to the things as they are.

374
00:33:40,772 --> 00:33:41,833
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know it, you know it.

375
00:33:41,893 --> 00:33:47,976
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep asking yourself or reminding yourself whether you know... Well, you have to do that in the beginning.

376
00:33:48,116 --> 00:33:48,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sorry.

377
00:33:49,637 --> 00:33:50,958
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to do that in the beginning.

378
00:33:51,534 --> 00:33:52,674
[SPEAKER_01]: But later on, you don't.

379
00:33:52,874 --> 00:33:53,775
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge is there.

380
00:33:54,975 --> 00:33:55,495
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

381
00:33:55,535 --> 00:34:05,459
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, you will have to stop doing whatever you were doing in order to remind yourself of the, you know, the order of mindfulness and so on.

382
00:34:06,579 --> 00:34:17,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do that only so that, okay, now I reminded myself, now let me continue doing what I was doing and try to remain mindful with that one percent of the background, as I spoke before, for as long as I can.

383
00:34:17,823 --> 00:34:19,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Vipassana is completely the opposite.

384
00:34:20,096 --> 00:34:25,821
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are not... Stop being mindful of that background and just focus as hard as you can to whatever is right in front of you.

385
00:34:25,981 --> 00:34:27,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Not seeing the background.

386
00:34:27,163 --> 00:34:27,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

387
00:34:27,383 --> 00:34:28,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically like...

388
00:34:28,844 --> 00:34:30,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine, I don't know, what would be the simile?

389
00:34:30,986 --> 00:34:31,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

390
00:34:32,427 --> 00:34:33,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Fishing rod.

391
00:34:33,268 --> 00:34:39,494
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, you throw the fishing rod into the water and then you have to wait for the fish to grab the bait.

392
00:34:40,275 --> 00:34:46,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, vipassana would be just you throw the rod, you take it out, you throw it, you take it out, you throw it, you take it out, and that becomes a goal in itself.

393
00:34:47,947 --> 00:34:49,048
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you don't want to do.

394
00:34:49,528 --> 00:34:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, sometimes you have to attend back to the things, to the reasoning, to the thoughts, to what you've read based on mindfulness.

395
00:34:57,095 --> 00:35:00,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Only so to leave it and see if it's going to settle the proper way.

396
00:35:03,241 --> 00:35:05,943
[SPEAKER_01]: When you forget about it, then okay, remind yourself again.

397
00:35:07,064 --> 00:35:12,969
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing, like, you don't want to have to keep it right in front of your mind all the time, because that's impossible.

398
00:35:14,475 --> 00:35:20,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be able to let it go, like, seep into the background and still be aware of it.

399
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:23,382
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how far it goes, you're aware to that extent.

400
00:35:24,523 --> 00:35:25,343
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're still aware.

401
00:35:25,984 --> 00:35:33,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why even 1% of such mindfulness is actually more than enough for... to be fully established in the aperture of mindfulness.

402
00:35:34,450 --> 00:35:40,855
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually there for means to be fully established, to not have 99% of your attention needing for... to be mindful.

403
00:35:40,895 --> 00:35:41,095
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

404
00:35:41,455 --> 00:35:43,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Because mindfulness is a structural thing.

405
00:35:43,957 --> 00:35:46,079
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you do or have to do.

406
00:35:46,540 --> 00:35:48,241
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, yes, because you don't know what it is.

407
00:35:48,982 --> 00:35:52,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you discern it, knowing what mindfulness is, is what makes you mindful.

408
00:35:54,347 --> 00:36:02,194
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose if I'm mindful of the bodily posture, if it's in my background, is it the same?

409
00:36:02,675 --> 00:36:04,817
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, you can be mindful of it, actually.

410
00:36:05,184 --> 00:36:14,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you're thinking about your positive posture, then, it's... your body is always the background, but now you're also thinking about it, so it's present in both ways.

411
00:36:15,627 --> 00:36:23,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But sooner or later, you have to stop thinking about it, and when you do, you still want to be able to remain aware of it as a background.

412
00:36:24,711 --> 00:36:25,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Be aware.

413
00:36:25,631 --> 00:36:25,871
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

414
00:36:26,171 --> 00:36:26,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is there.

415
00:36:27,091 --> 00:36:28,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

416
00:36:28,112 --> 00:36:28,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body is there.

417
00:36:29,032 --> 00:36:35,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you forget about it, it is there, in a certain position, in a certain posture, doing something.

418
00:36:36,220 --> 00:36:38,241
[SPEAKER_01]: And if somebody was to ask you, how is your body now?

419
00:36:38,281 --> 00:36:39,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to give the answer right away.

420
00:36:40,723 --> 00:36:42,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the knowledge of that is there.

421
00:36:43,565 --> 00:36:44,946
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just not being recognized.

422
00:36:46,227 --> 00:36:49,369
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'm aware of that, it means that already it's in front.

423
00:36:50,050 --> 00:36:50,230
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

424
00:36:50,350 --> 00:36:52,892
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're aware, you can be aware for where it is.

425
00:36:53,032 --> 00:36:55,413
[SPEAKER_01]: If you attend it, then you make it in front.

426
00:36:57,135 --> 00:37:00,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whether you're attending something or not, you're aware of it.

427
00:37:00,925 --> 00:37:03,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, awareness is more fundamental than the attention, structurally.

428
00:37:03,987 --> 00:37:06,348
[SPEAKER_02]: It means that awareness is not a mode.

429
00:37:07,488 --> 00:37:08,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I don't notice.

430
00:37:08,449 --> 00:37:10,890
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not noticing awareness, then you're unaware.

431
00:37:11,430 --> 00:37:11,971
[SPEAKER_02]: Unaware.

432
00:37:12,211 --> 00:37:15,312
[SPEAKER_01]: But awareness as a potential is there.

433
00:37:16,133 --> 00:37:18,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you weren't, it means you wouldn't wake up.

434
00:37:18,194 --> 00:37:19,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to become aware.

435
00:37:20,375 --> 00:37:22,796
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the difference between awareness and consciousness?

436
00:37:23,716 --> 00:37:28,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let's say the consciousness is that direction given to you.

437
00:37:31,145 --> 00:37:33,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Direction that comes from that random body.

438
00:37:33,627 --> 00:37:35,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Both present all the time?

439
00:37:36,428 --> 00:37:36,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

440
00:37:36,729 --> 00:37:38,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Awareness is present all the time?

441
00:37:38,670 --> 00:37:43,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, awareness is probable all the time.

442
00:37:45,094 --> 00:37:46,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It is potential all the time.

443
00:37:46,915 --> 00:37:49,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't know it, then you're unaware.

444
00:37:50,177 --> 00:37:50,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Unaware.

445
00:37:51,078 --> 00:37:53,159
[SPEAKER_02]: But you said I'm already aware of the background.

446
00:37:53,179 --> 00:37:55,941
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're always in the direction, which means there is always a background.

447
00:37:56,621 --> 00:37:58,082
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a direction of something,

448
00:37:58,803 --> 00:38:03,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, it has been directed from some point and that's the background to where it is.

449
00:38:05,165 --> 00:38:11,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is always that direction, that background, but that doesn't...

450
00:38:11,406 --> 00:38:18,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always possible for you to recognize it, to know it, to be mindful of it, but it doesn't mean that you always are mindful of it.

451
00:38:19,008 --> 00:38:25,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the point of the practice of dhamma.

452
00:38:25,330 --> 00:38:26,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Where can we get this?

453
00:38:27,842 --> 00:38:35,569
[SPEAKER_02]: To see the background is important, because... because I have to see the self, from where... from where I'm attending.

454
00:38:35,909 --> 00:38:41,394
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is... No, you don't wanna... you can't see yourself directly, and then say, this is not myself.

455
00:38:42,195 --> 00:38:54,165
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to start including everything, actual and peripheral, foreground and the background, and if you do that long enough, then you will start to discern that your back... your sense of self can't be in between the two.

456
00:38:54,865 --> 00:38:58,346
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either the foreground or background, and as such, it can't be permanent.

457
00:38:59,407 --> 00:39:02,628
[SPEAKER_01]: It's by not knowing that, you assume yourself to be in limbo.

458
00:39:02,668 --> 00:39:06,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, neither foreground or background, it's everywhere and nowhere.

459
00:39:07,770 --> 00:39:11,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only because you don't really know either foreground or the background.

460
00:39:12,312 --> 00:39:13,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the experience.

461
00:39:14,632 --> 00:39:15,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

462
00:39:15,413 --> 00:39:21,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Usually what... set of view is usually standing background, but it's not seen.

463
00:39:22,095 --> 00:39:23,536
[SPEAKER_02]: But before I remember, I...

464
00:39:25,306 --> 00:39:37,433
[SPEAKER_02]: In the meditation, usually, I see that the whole picture of the mind, what is, of course, being mindful of the body, but at the same time, in the mind, what kind of thoughts are arising?

465
00:39:37,553 --> 00:39:38,133
[SPEAKER_02]: I see them.

466
00:39:38,153 --> 00:39:47,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I remember that I have to see the whole picture, the whole picture that I'm taking in this mind, means me or myself, who I am.

467
00:39:47,923 --> 00:39:49,904
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have to see the whole picture.

468
00:39:50,684 --> 00:39:53,745
[SPEAKER_02]: No need to focus on the one thought itself.

469
00:39:53,785 --> 00:40:00,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but see, like again, you're sort of repeating what I said, but you're not saying it in the same way.

470
00:40:00,347 --> 00:40:05,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And the difference is, again, that being mindful of the background means being mindful of your experience as a whole.

471
00:40:06,109 --> 00:40:11,711
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you say, I have to see the whole picture, that means I have to attend the whole picture.

472
00:40:12,171 --> 00:40:14,111
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're trying to put it all in front of you.

473
00:40:15,372 --> 00:40:16,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not what I'm saying.

474
00:40:17,381 --> 00:40:22,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no matter how much you manage to put in front of you, there's always going to be background to it.

475
00:40:23,303 --> 00:40:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't recognize it, means you have left quite a good bit out.

476
00:40:27,105 --> 00:40:28,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where yourself is going to be then.

477
00:40:30,146 --> 00:40:40,090
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start seeing, okay, this is actual, this is what I'm doing, while with the back of my mind, I'm mindful of myself doing that, which is already a formal background for what you're doing,

478
00:40:41,640 --> 00:40:47,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, you recognize that there is no room for yourself to be anywhere in between, or left, or... it will be either here or there.

479
00:40:47,702 --> 00:40:56,205
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, it will pertain to either here or there, and you won't be that sense of independent self.

480
00:40:56,265 --> 00:41:00,927
[SPEAKER_02]: It means that... it means the purpose of the... your practice is that... to find out.

481
00:41:00,987 --> 00:41:03,388
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing else you can find that as a self.

482
00:41:03,588 --> 00:41:10,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose that if I'm mindful of the body... To find out that your sense of self, basically, is not independent.

483
00:41:11,479 --> 00:41:16,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Because at the moment, for a Pratyekabuddhist, the sense of self feels independent of anything and everything.

484
00:41:17,942 --> 00:41:33,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by always practicing mindfulness, well, by developing the extent of mindfulness for as long as it's necessary, then it can become apparent that even that sense of self that appears, that can be talked about, can be designated,

485
00:41:33,898 --> 00:41:34,818
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not independent.

486
00:41:35,139 --> 00:41:36,719
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be...

487
00:41:36,779 --> 00:41:51,505
[SPEAKER_01]: It's depending upon this reason background and these things that I'm directly attending, whatever they may be, sweeping, washing dishes, reading books, whatever, while your body, randomly living, is present there, your senses working and so on.

488
00:41:51,525 --> 00:41:58,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize the sense of self is usually was hiding kind of in between by not knowing either of those things.

489
00:41:58,228 --> 00:42:01,889
[SPEAKER_01]: But now that you're practicing mindfulness correctly in regard to the experience as a whole,

490
00:42:02,377 --> 00:42:07,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, sooner or later, that sense of self will lose that gratuitous position of independence.

491
00:42:07,980 --> 00:42:11,402
[SPEAKER_01]: And you just... well, even if there is sense of self, it's not mine.

492
00:42:12,122 --> 00:42:13,883
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be mine.

493
00:42:13,943 --> 00:42:15,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what not-self means.

494
00:42:16,945 --> 00:42:17,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I said it before.

495
00:42:17,725 --> 00:42:19,066
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean there is no self.

496
00:42:19,126 --> 00:42:22,208
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that that which is taken as self is not yours.

497
00:42:22,708 --> 00:42:23,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be yours.

498
00:42:25,069 --> 00:42:25,669
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

499
00:42:25,689 --> 00:42:29,431
[SPEAKER_01]: It, it, it... Because sometimes we are... this is not myself or something like that.

500
00:42:29,952 --> 00:42:32,173
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not how you overcome the sense of self.

501
00:42:32,833 --> 00:42:40,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You overcome that by knowing that that cannot even be your sense of self and could not have been your sense of self.

502
00:42:40,435 --> 00:42:41,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it won't be your sense of self.

503
00:42:43,835 --> 00:42:44,475
[SPEAKER_02]: This is...

504
00:42:44,816 --> 00:42:52,417
[SPEAKER_02]: So, while you're moving, you are usually just maintaining mindfulness that, I am doing this stuff, I am washing the cup, or... you're thinking...

505
00:42:52,437 --> 00:42:53,177
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to know.

506
00:42:53,197 --> 00:43:00,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... you can be washing the cup or whatever, but you don't want to have to be telling yourself, I am washing the cup, I am washing the cup.

507
00:43:00,719 --> 00:43:01,419
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to know

508
00:43:02,033 --> 00:43:07,416
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be seeing yourself, with the back of your mind, seeing yourself washing the cup.

509
00:43:10,598 --> 00:43:12,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... let's say... let's put it like this.

510
00:43:12,760 --> 00:43:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bit more relaxed than needing to constantly attend it.

511
00:43:16,702 --> 00:43:17,502
[SPEAKER_01]: I am washing the cup.

512
00:43:17,643 --> 00:43:18,423
[SPEAKER_01]: I am washing the cup.

513
00:43:18,503 --> 00:43:19,324
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm holding the cup.

514
00:43:20,224 --> 00:43:20,364
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

515
00:43:20,384 --> 00:43:21,545
[SPEAKER_01]: You already picked up the cup.

516
00:43:21,665 --> 00:43:23,586
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep telling to yourself what you're doing.

517
00:43:23,666 --> 00:43:24,547
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what you're doing.

518
00:43:25,427 --> 00:43:31,291
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just want to... almost like... like in... almost imagine...

519
00:43:32,128 --> 00:43:40,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Another view looking at you doing this, but not interfering, not telling you anything, just keeping an eye on what you're doing.

520
00:43:40,779 --> 00:43:42,200
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the practice of reflection.

521
00:43:43,502 --> 00:43:47,387
[SPEAKER_01]: You just reflect upon yourself doing whatever you happen to be doing.

522
00:43:49,978 --> 00:43:58,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but that's the thing.

523
00:43:58,743 --> 00:44:03,005
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start keeping the observer, you start assuming that observer to be your more real self.

524
00:44:04,066 --> 00:44:04,546
[SPEAKER_01]: But no.

525
00:44:04,766 --> 00:44:09,509
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you can reflect upon yourself doing something is because you're doing something.

526
00:44:09,529 --> 00:44:10,709
[SPEAKER_01]: You're able to do something.

527
00:44:12,150 --> 00:44:15,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that particular thing you're doing is the reason, or rather,

528
00:44:16,449 --> 00:44:28,314
[SPEAKER_01]: That particular body that you are using in doing these things is the reason that you are alive, that you are able to think and do these things and discern them as something to be done.

529
00:44:29,215 --> 00:44:39,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how far you go in your reflection and your observer or whatever else, that will never be independent of this living body right here that is washing the dishes.

530
00:44:39,620 --> 00:44:40,460
[SPEAKER_01]: And vice versa.

531
00:44:40,500 --> 00:44:44,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Washing the dishes will never be intelligible or known if there is no reflection

532
00:44:45,646 --> 00:44:46,947
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to it.

533
00:44:47,648 --> 00:44:49,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, nāma-rūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāma-rūpa.

534
00:44:50,390 --> 00:44:52,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Mutually determining each other like the two sticks.

535
00:44:52,532 --> 00:44:53,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You move one, the other one falls.

536
00:44:54,153 --> 00:44:54,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Instantly.

537
00:44:57,055 --> 00:44:59,057
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that anchor, is anything of it?

538
00:44:59,077 --> 00:45:00,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Like a background?

539
00:45:00,158 --> 00:45:01,619
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends on the person.

540
00:45:02,019 --> 00:45:06,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people, they like to keep an anchor in some, like, bread or something.

541
00:45:06,183 --> 00:45:07,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Is anything of it, or...?

542
00:45:07,604 --> 00:45:11,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, everything is... anything can be an anchor, but not...

543
00:45:12,377 --> 00:45:17,861
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't necessarily mean that people can actually establish an anchor of mindfulness in everything.

544
00:45:18,981 --> 00:45:19,962
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the purpose of anchor?

545
00:45:19,982 --> 00:45:22,603
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, they just make it... Well, first of all, anchor means mindfulness.

546
00:45:22,984 --> 00:45:24,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Anchored in that means you're mindful of that.

547
00:45:25,145 --> 00:45:28,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You are reflecting upon yourself while you're washing the dishes.

548
00:45:28,086 --> 00:45:30,468
[SPEAKER_01]: You're mindful of the background while you're washing the dishes.

549
00:45:31,388 --> 00:45:34,450
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say mindful of the background, I don't mean mindful of different things.

550
00:45:34,990 --> 00:45:39,313
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean like I'm washing the dishes, but I'm listening to this sound, which, because of that, I'm mindful.

551
00:45:39,353 --> 00:45:39,513
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

552
00:45:40,290 --> 00:45:43,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Washing the dishes means you're mindful of your body washing the dishes.

553
00:45:45,955 --> 00:45:48,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're mindful of standing.

554
00:45:48,217 --> 00:45:52,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You're mindful of the given body while you're doing particular dishes with your hands and so on.

555
00:45:53,902 --> 00:45:57,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can even be mindful of the intention to wash the dishes.

556
00:45:58,486 --> 00:46:03,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for as long as your intention to have the dishes washed is present, you'll be washing the dishes.

557
00:46:05,152 --> 00:46:07,454
[SPEAKER_01]: When that intention changes, you'll stop doing it.

558
00:46:08,363 --> 00:46:10,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Either because you finished, or because something more important came up.

559
00:46:12,006 --> 00:46:12,627
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing.

560
00:46:13,367 --> 00:46:15,129
[SPEAKER_01]: What people do, they forget, they don't see that.

561
00:46:15,149 --> 00:46:21,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have the intention to wash the dishes, and you start washing the dishes, and you forgot about the intention to wash the dishes.

562
00:46:21,934 --> 00:46:26,178
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually still present there, just now, in that background, on a more general level.

563
00:46:26,978 --> 00:46:27,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

564
00:46:27,339 --> 00:46:34,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were not present there, you wouldn't be picking up the sponge, taking more, washing up liquid, getting another cup,

565
00:46:35,698 --> 00:46:43,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is bound by a more general intention of having these dishes washed, having this sink clean.

566
00:46:44,483 --> 00:46:52,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're mindful of that more general intention, that is still there, so you don't have to create it, fabricate it, you just have to discern it, because it's there.

567
00:46:54,008 --> 00:46:58,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you'll be mindful of whatever particular things you do on account of that intention.

568
00:47:01,372 --> 00:47:02,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's just...

569
00:47:02,765 --> 00:47:10,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And that intention and so on, it's another thought, but it's a more subtle thought than the actual thoughts you're having while you're washing the dishes.

570
00:47:10,228 --> 00:47:25,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It means if I'm aware... my mindfulness is on the bodily posture, then being aware of the intention... You can be mindful of your bodily posture and then don't interfere with your thoughts and what you're doing while you're mindful of your bodily posture.

571
00:47:26,513 --> 00:47:27,594
[SPEAKER_01]: That's maybe simpler.

572
00:47:29,354 --> 00:47:30,635
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know whatever I'm doing.

573
00:47:31,389 --> 00:47:35,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you're doing, you can do only because your body is still there, alive.

574
00:47:36,533 --> 00:47:38,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses are working and functioning.

575
00:47:39,395 --> 00:47:56,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're mindful of that body, bodily posture, more specifically, and then allow yourself, allow your mind to go and do things, while peripherally you do, like, keep a corner of your eye, so to speak, on that bodily posture while you're doing this stuff.

576
00:47:57,515 --> 00:48:01,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will already be a more accurate practice of mindfulness.

577
00:48:02,518 --> 00:48:05,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be over-intending, over-attending things.

578
00:48:05,500 --> 00:48:07,822
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be, I'm washing the dishes, I'm washing...

579
00:48:07,862 --> 00:48:09,323
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm standing, I'm standing, I'm standing.

580
00:48:09,503 --> 00:48:16,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're just bringing things forward forcefully, which means you'll never ever see that background and there won't be any anchor established in it.

581
00:48:17,788 --> 00:48:26,234
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize that this standing body, or sitting body, or like this body in itself, for as long as it's alive,

582
00:48:34,306 --> 00:48:49,492
[SPEAKER_01]: If those elements break apart, if they're taken away, and they will be, because I have no idea where they came from, how they're given, or how can they even be... well, they can't even be accessed in any way, but they are the reason for your whole experience as a whole.

583
00:48:50,613 --> 00:48:55,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when those four great elements go, you can't even conceive the experience to remain standing.

584
00:48:56,789 --> 00:48:59,790
[SPEAKER_02]: How about if you just simply do body contemplation?

585
00:49:00,370 --> 00:49:02,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, if you do it correctly, it's fine.

586
00:49:03,211 --> 00:49:10,053
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're doing body contemplation in terms of just bringing things forward into your mind, while not seeing the background, you won't be mindful of it.

587
00:49:11,293 --> 00:49:13,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Body contemplation, how to see the background?

588
00:49:13,254 --> 00:49:23,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, body contemplation, the correct one in the suttas, it's just recognizing the random parts of the body as that because of which you're able to even think about random parts of your body.

589
00:49:23,897 --> 00:49:25,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Taking the body as mind.

590
00:49:25,889 --> 00:49:26,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the result of it.

591
00:49:29,132 --> 00:49:34,576
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all feeling.

592
00:49:35,537 --> 00:49:38,919
[SPEAKER_01]: The sensation they talk about is just the bodily perceptions.

593
00:49:39,640 --> 00:49:47,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, by calling them feelings, they kind of give them a more prominent position, which result, if you can observe that long enough, you'll be purified.

594
00:49:47,846 --> 00:49:49,268
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's only the contradiction in turn.

595
00:49:49,288 --> 00:49:49,648
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no

596
00:49:50,310 --> 00:49:53,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplating on the perception of changing the impulse on a positive way.

597
00:49:53,153 --> 00:49:53,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

598
00:49:53,353 --> 00:49:53,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that relevant?

599
00:49:53,994 --> 00:49:55,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it relevant how my elbow feels?

600
00:49:55,115 --> 00:49:55,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

601
00:50:12,548 --> 00:50:13,208
[SPEAKER_01]: My knees hurt.

602
00:50:13,249 --> 00:50:13,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So what?

603
00:50:14,009 --> 00:50:17,832
[SPEAKER_02]: But for Putujan, the main problem is you're taking the body as mind.

604
00:50:17,872 --> 00:50:18,512
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

605
00:50:18,672 --> 00:50:22,114
[SPEAKER_01]: The main problem is not... he doesn't know the way out of unpleasant feeling.

606
00:50:22,555 --> 00:50:23,235
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the main problem.

607
00:50:23,255 --> 00:50:24,456
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Putujan is Putujan.

608
00:50:24,496 --> 00:50:26,497
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know four normal truths.

609
00:50:27,978 --> 00:50:31,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is the thing... You can stare at his body for the eternity.

610
00:50:32,902 --> 00:50:34,343
[SPEAKER_01]: He's looking in the wrong place.

611
00:50:35,503 --> 00:50:39,086
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not why and how I feel this body and how it changes and how it moves.

612
00:50:39,763 --> 00:50:45,688
[SPEAKER_01]: It's why do I not know escape from the feelings that arise dependent on this body?

613
00:50:46,649 --> 00:50:47,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Any feelings.

614
00:50:48,310 --> 00:50:50,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't they escape because taking the body as mind?

615
00:50:51,133 --> 00:50:53,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they can't escape because they don't know what escape is.

616
00:50:55,436 --> 00:51:07,246
[SPEAKER_02]: But if someone really, like, contemplates that this body, that element, four elements are changing... Again, if they're doing it right in front of them, over-attending it, it will not stand for that which elements are.

617
00:51:07,828 --> 00:51:09,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Because elements are always the background.

618
00:51:10,570 --> 00:51:17,115
[SPEAKER_01]: The four great elements can be discerned and recognized in that very distant background that is present.

619
00:51:17,235 --> 00:51:19,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there and now, but it's much, much, much subtler.

620
00:51:20,698 --> 00:51:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And unless your attention has been trained to the subtle levels, you just won't be able to see it.

621
00:51:27,203 --> 00:51:35,009
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like, ah, well, imagine you have a lens, camera lens or a pair of glasses, whatever, that it's only for a certain distance.

622
00:51:35,567 --> 00:51:39,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Like 50 millimeters to 80 millimeter distance, which means anything before that you won't see.

623
00:51:41,008 --> 00:51:43,829
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how close you look, you're just overseeing it.

624
00:51:45,409 --> 00:51:47,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And that that's basically untrained attention.

625
00:51:48,930 --> 00:51:50,910
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just used to over attending things.

626
00:51:50,970 --> 00:51:52,691
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that's what you're used to regarding.

627
00:51:52,711 --> 00:51:54,151
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you're able to recognize.

628
00:51:54,611 --> 00:51:55,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything less than that, you won't see.

629
00:51:56,431 --> 00:51:59,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person with such attention, even if they

630
00:52:00,053 --> 00:52:01,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about elements or whatever.

631
00:52:01,293 --> 00:52:02,773
[SPEAKER_01]: They won't be thinking about elements.

632
00:52:02,793 --> 00:52:09,135
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll think about that which they think elements are, based on that scope of attention that they have.

633
00:52:10,655 --> 00:52:20,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start training your attention in regard to the background, you start seeing more of that direction without actually having to change it.

634
00:52:21,997 --> 00:52:22,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

635
00:52:22,757 --> 00:52:26,358
[SPEAKER_02]: How to contemplate on the impermanence of the body?

636
00:52:26,858 --> 00:52:28,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, if you...

637
00:52:28,915 --> 00:52:48,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body, right there, right now, citta, whatever, you have saved its position and you have saved more particular things, but overall, that body aging, that body growing, that body getting sick, that body being subject to impermanence, you can't stop that.

638
00:52:49,347 --> 00:52:53,030
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive how it would be if you could control it.

639
00:52:53,050 --> 00:52:54,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inaccessible to you.

640
00:52:57,272 --> 00:52:58,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you clarify that,

641
00:52:59,014 --> 00:53:05,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can start seeing that body as actually a reason for your whole life, for your whole experience, for everything.

642
00:53:06,535 --> 00:53:12,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything you've ever experienced, everything you will ever experience, is because your body was there living with senses functioning.

643
00:53:15,077 --> 00:53:18,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your experience can't remain standing without that body.

644
00:53:18,997 --> 00:53:23,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the impermanence is not looking at the change, because change is just another experience.

645
00:53:25,058 --> 00:53:28,479
[SPEAKER_01]: The impermanence is recognizing that inaccessibility, like

646
00:53:29,254 --> 00:53:31,555
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing has arisen on its own.

647
00:53:32,515 --> 00:53:34,236
[SPEAKER_01]: That body is there on its own, really.

648
00:53:34,356 --> 00:53:37,537
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't have to say it's creation, it's manifestation in the senses or anything.

649
00:53:37,557 --> 00:53:38,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just using it.

650
00:53:39,898 --> 00:53:42,700
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can only use it because it was already given beforehand.

651
00:53:43,160 --> 00:53:44,660
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what impermanence means.

652
00:53:45,421 --> 00:53:48,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, this using won't last forever.

653
00:53:48,642 --> 00:53:48,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

654
00:53:48,942 --> 00:53:52,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Because where these things came from is inconceivable to me.

655
00:53:52,504 --> 00:53:57,966
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't even imagine where and how the elements, whatever, but you can't even access it.

656
00:53:58,653 --> 00:54:00,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, it's impermanent.

657
00:54:01,014 --> 00:54:03,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because... not because you can see it being destroyed.

658
00:54:04,857 --> 00:54:06,398
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to do with impermanence.

659
00:54:06,418 --> 00:54:10,600
[SPEAKER_02]: He has to see that it is there, where the body or thought or whatever.

660
00:54:10,661 --> 00:54:13,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Only has to see... no need to see any change.

661
00:54:13,622 --> 00:54:15,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Only has to see that it is there.

662
00:54:15,824 --> 00:54:17,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, you want to not see the change.

663
00:54:18,465 --> 00:54:20,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Because change is always on a more particular level.

664
00:54:21,387 --> 00:54:23,729
[SPEAKER_01]: While thing remains the same on a more general level.

665
00:54:24,109 --> 00:54:25,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you want to start distinguishing.

666
00:54:27,270 --> 00:54:30,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for as long as you're going after change, your mind is going with it.

667
00:54:31,073 --> 00:54:33,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not seeing, you're not seeing the nature of that.

668
00:54:34,836 --> 00:54:39,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whether the thing is changing or not, there has to be a degree of that thing staying the same.

669
00:54:42,163 --> 00:54:43,844
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's always on a more general level.

670
00:54:44,304 --> 00:54:44,625
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all.

671
00:54:44,645 --> 00:54:47,247
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always in that background.

672
00:54:48,530 --> 00:54:57,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Some putuṣṭhāna, I think, some putuṣṭhāna, they don't have identification with the body, but some just identify with the mental aggregates only.

673
00:54:57,632 --> 00:54:59,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, theoretically, that's possible.

674
00:54:59,933 --> 00:55:00,713
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said that.

675
00:55:01,253 --> 00:55:06,554
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think nowadays, it's more... every putuṣṭhāna is identified with the body and everything.

676
00:55:08,514 --> 00:55:15,296
[SPEAKER_01]: If putuṣṭhāna were to have that much wisdom to truly, truly abandon his body for what it is,

677
00:55:16,208 --> 00:55:32,565
[SPEAKER_01]: With all of the instruction today, and all the suttas, and coming across the dhamma in the Buddhist teaching, you won't need much more to then discern the general nature of the mind and any other phenomena as impermanent and not being able to hold it as his own self.

678
00:55:35,232 --> 00:55:37,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, most likely, the putujana...

679
00:55:37,274 --> 00:55:41,717
[SPEAKER_02]: It's possible that a hundred percent never identifies the body for putujana.

680
00:55:41,758 --> 00:55:45,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it possible to do that, to suffer through any jihājā, practicing?

681
00:55:45,481 --> 00:55:48,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, theoretically, it's possible, but it would still mean a lot of wisdom.

682
00:55:50,605 --> 00:55:58,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's quite unlikely that a person today will develop so much wisdom and not come across the Buddha's teachings to kind of get the final right tune or the right view.

683
00:56:00,653 --> 00:56:02,635
[SPEAKER_02]: For such putujana, only has to...

684
00:56:04,151 --> 00:56:09,012
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no different mental and bodily aggregates.

685
00:56:09,072 --> 00:56:11,793
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience as a whole, it's experience as a whole.

686
00:56:12,393 --> 00:56:15,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body wouldn't be known as your body if it's not already appearing on a mental level.

687
00:56:17,255 --> 00:56:20,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Your thoughts wouldn't be known as your thoughts if your living body is not already there.

688
00:56:21,616 --> 00:56:28,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that distinction between body and my mind, it's kind of unnecessary.

689
00:56:30,140 --> 00:56:34,002
